WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.058 --> 00:00:04.247
- What's up, believe nation?

00:00:04.247 --> 00:00:07.683
It's Evan, my one word is
believe, and I believe in people

00:00:07.683 --> 00:00:09.959
more than they believe in themselves.

00:00:09.959 --> 00:00:11.241
And my sincere hope is that

00:00:11.241 --> 00:00:14.286
if you see in yourself what I see in you,

00:00:14.286 --> 00:00:17.140
you'll be able to go off
and change the planet.

00:00:17.140 --> 00:00:18.531
Now we started the Mentor Me series

00:00:18.531 --> 00:00:21.550
to try to learn from people
who've done a lot more than us.

00:00:21.550 --> 00:00:23.391
And by hanging around them,

00:00:23.391 --> 00:00:25.359
and absorbing their wisdom
and their knowledge.

00:00:25.359 --> 00:00:27.046
some of their mindsets, their beliefs,

00:00:27.046 --> 00:00:29.017
their attitudes, their
way of seeing the world,

00:00:29.017 --> 00:00:31.066
seep into us to help us become

00:00:31.066 --> 00:00:32.954
the best version of ourselves.

00:00:32.954 --> 00:00:34.758
So today we're going to
learn from Simon Sinek

00:00:34.758 --> 00:00:36.717
and how to start with Why.

00:00:36.717 --> 00:00:38.405
Mentor me, Simon.

00:00:38.405 --> 00:00:41.572
(inspirational music)

00:00:51.560 --> 00:00:54.143
- The Why was born out of pain.

00:00:55.840 --> 00:00:58.396
It was never an academic
or commercial exercise,

00:00:58.396 --> 00:01:01.154
it was born at a time in my life

00:01:01.154 --> 00:01:03.359
many years ago, ten years ago,

00:01:03.359 --> 00:01:06.361
where I had lost my passion
for what I was doing.

00:01:06.361 --> 00:01:07.768
I owned my own small business,

00:01:07.768 --> 00:01:09.448
I was living the American dream.

00:01:09.448 --> 00:01:11.763
Superficially, my life was fantastic.

00:01:11.763 --> 00:01:14.234
And yet, I didn't want to
wake up and do it again,

00:01:14.234 --> 00:01:15.906
and I was embarrassed by that.

00:01:15.906 --> 00:01:17.504
You know, who am I to
complain about my life?

00:01:17.504 --> 00:01:20.309
My life seemed perfect and yet I hated it,

00:01:20.309 --> 00:01:22.212
and so I kept it to myself.

00:01:22.212 --> 00:01:25.127
Every ounce of my being,
all the energy that I had

00:01:25.127 --> 00:01:27.806
was invested pretending
that I was happier,

00:01:27.806 --> 00:01:31.508
more successful, and more
in control than I felt.

00:01:31.508 --> 00:01:34.438
And it was debilitating, quite frankly.

00:01:34.438 --> 00:01:36.332
Strange things start to
happen when you put yourself

00:01:36.332 --> 00:01:38.665
in that cycle and the
stress starts to build.

00:01:38.665 --> 00:01:40.006
You start to become paranoid.

00:01:40.006 --> 00:01:44.054
So for example, I was convinced
that my employees hated me.

00:01:44.054 --> 00:01:47.545
I didn't go out much, and it was really,

00:01:47.545 --> 00:01:49.000
it was really a bad time.

00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:51.892
It was a confluence of events,

00:01:51.892 --> 00:01:54.052
and I made this discovery

00:01:54.052 --> 00:01:56.416
that every single
organization on the planet,

00:01:56.416 --> 00:01:57.631
even our own careers,

00:01:57.631 --> 00:01:59.294
always function on the same three levels.

00:01:59.294 --> 00:02:01.153
What we do, how we do
it, and why we do it.

00:02:01.153 --> 00:02:03.486
And it was based on the biology
of human decision making.

00:02:03.486 --> 00:02:06.293
It wasn't some highfalutin
management theory,

00:02:06.293 --> 00:02:08.691
it was based on brain stuff.

00:02:08.691 --> 00:02:10.401
And I realized I knew what I did,

00:02:10.401 --> 00:02:13.031
I was good at it, and I knew how I did it.

00:02:13.031 --> 00:02:14.064
I could tell you what was different

00:02:14.064 --> 00:02:16.107
or special about the way I did things,

00:02:16.107 --> 00:02:18.147
but I couldn't tell
you why I was doing it.

00:02:18.147 --> 00:02:20.344
That was the missing piece,
you have to have all three.

00:02:20.344 --> 00:02:23.018
I became obsessed with
this thing called the Why.

00:02:23.018 --> 00:02:25.433
I figured out how to find my
Why and it restored my passion

00:02:25.433 --> 00:02:28.816
to levels I had never experienced before.

00:02:28.816 --> 00:02:30.637
And more importantly, I figured out

00:02:30.637 --> 00:02:32.546
how to help others find theirs.

00:02:32.546 --> 00:02:34.091
And I did what anyone would do.

00:02:34.091 --> 00:02:35.401
When you discover something beautiful,

00:02:35.401 --> 00:02:36.912
you share it with your friends.

00:02:36.912 --> 00:02:39.471
And my friends started making
crazy life changes themselves,

00:02:39.471 --> 00:02:41.593
and they started finding
happiness and passion

00:02:41.593 --> 00:02:43.351
that they'd never experienced before.

00:02:43.351 --> 00:02:45.424
It was me solving my own problem,

00:02:45.424 --> 00:02:47.866
that happened to help others
solve it for them, too.

00:02:47.866 --> 00:02:51.552
And people just kept inviting
me and I just kept saying yes.

00:02:51.552 --> 00:02:54.802
I was making huge decisions
that were really easy to make.

00:02:54.802 --> 00:02:56.968
Like I shut down my office
and started over again

00:02:56.968 --> 00:02:58.892
because I realized the
business I had built

00:02:58.892 --> 00:03:00.962
was so inconsistent with my Why.

00:03:00.962 --> 00:03:02.462
All my friends thought
I went out of business

00:03:02.462 --> 00:03:03.616
and they were worried about me.

00:03:03.616 --> 00:03:06.413
It was the easiest decision I ever made.

00:03:06.413 --> 00:03:09.107
In other words, when you know
your Why the filter is clear.

00:03:09.107 --> 00:03:10.756
It's not like there are options.

00:03:10.756 --> 00:03:13.039
The option is obvious,
there's only one option.

00:03:13.039 --> 00:03:15.530
Share, give, inspire.

00:03:15.530 --> 00:03:18.080
As it turns out, there's a pattern.

00:03:18.080 --> 00:03:21.630
As it turns out, all the
great and inspiring leaders

00:03:21.630 --> 00:03:23.314
and organizations in the
world, whether it's Apple,

00:03:23.314 --> 00:03:25.164
or Martin Luther King,
or the Wright brothers.

00:03:25.164 --> 00:03:29.963
They all think, act, and
communicate the exact same way,

00:03:29.963 --> 00:03:33.997
and it's the complete
opposite to everyone else.

00:03:33.997 --> 00:03:36.047
All I did was codify it.

00:03:36.047 --> 00:03:39.724
And it's probably the
world's simplest idea.

00:03:39.724 --> 00:03:42.057
I call it The Golden Circle.

00:03:50.124 --> 00:03:52.514
Why, how, what.

00:03:52.514 --> 00:03:55.945
This little idea explains
why some organizations

00:03:55.945 --> 00:03:59.048
and some leaders are able to
inspire, where others aren't.

00:03:59.048 --> 00:04:01.310
Let me define the terms really quickly.

00:04:01.310 --> 00:04:03.320
Every single person,
every single organization

00:04:03.320 --> 00:04:07.056
on the planet knows what they do, 100%.

00:04:07.056 --> 00:04:09.371
Some know how they do it.

00:04:09.371 --> 00:04:11.545
Whether you call it your
differentiating value proposition

00:04:11.545 --> 00:04:14.320
or your proprietary process, or your USP,

00:04:14.320 --> 00:04:16.932
but very, very few people or organizations

00:04:16.932 --> 00:04:19.239
know why they do what they do.

00:04:19.239 --> 00:04:21.483
And by Why I don't mean to make a profit.

00:04:21.483 --> 00:04:23.718
That's a result, it's always a result.

00:04:23.718 --> 00:04:25.709
By Why I mean what's your purpose,

00:04:25.709 --> 00:04:27.880
what's your cause, what's your belief.

00:04:27.880 --> 00:04:30.630
Why does your organization exist?

00:04:31.493 --> 00:04:34.041
Why do you get out of bed in the morning,

00:04:34.041 --> 00:04:36.352
and why should anyone care?

00:04:36.352 --> 00:04:38.283
Well as a result, the way
we think, the way we act,

00:04:38.283 --> 00:04:40.902
the way we communicate is from
the outside in, it's obvious.

00:04:40.902 --> 00:04:43.898
We go from the clearest
thing to the fuzziest thing.

00:04:43.898 --> 00:04:47.958
But the inspired leaders and
the inspired organizations,

00:04:47.958 --> 00:04:51.184
regardless of their size,
regardless of their industry,

00:04:51.184 --> 00:04:55.351
all think, act, and communicate
from the inside out.

00:04:56.513 --> 00:04:59.128
Sometimes you can give
somebody all the facts

00:04:59.128 --> 00:05:00.260
and the figures, and they say,

00:05:00.260 --> 00:05:01.843
"I know what all the
facts and details say,

00:05:01.843 --> 00:05:04.185
"but it just doesn't feel right."

00:05:04.185 --> 00:05:06.222
Why would we use that verb,
"it doesn't feel right?"

00:05:06.222 --> 00:05:08.859
Because the part of the brain
that controls decision-making,

00:05:08.859 --> 00:05:10.259
doesn't control language.

00:05:10.259 --> 00:05:12.236
And the best we can muster
up is, "I don't know,

00:05:12.236 --> 00:05:14.221
"it just doesn't feel right."

00:05:14.221 --> 00:05:15.874
Or sometimes you say you're
leading with your heart

00:05:15.874 --> 00:05:17.211
or you're leading with your soul.

00:05:17.211 --> 00:05:18.492
Well, I hate to break it to you,

00:05:18.492 --> 00:05:20.995
those aren't the body parts
controlling your behavior.

00:05:20.995 --> 00:05:22.697
It's all happening here
in your limbic brain,

00:05:22.697 --> 00:05:25.289
the part of the brain that
controls decision making

00:05:25.289 --> 00:05:26.880
and not language.

00:05:26.880 --> 00:05:29.778
But if you don't know
why you do what you do,

00:05:29.778 --> 00:05:32.519
and people respond to
why you do what you do,

00:05:32.519 --> 00:05:35.596
then how will anybody, how
will you ever get people to,

00:05:35.596 --> 00:05:37.618
to vote for you or buy something from you,

00:05:37.618 --> 00:05:39.960
or more importantly, be loyal,

00:05:39.960 --> 00:05:43.075
and want to be a part of
what it is that you do.

00:05:43.075 --> 00:05:45.143
Again, the goal is not just to sell people

00:05:45.143 --> 00:05:46.654
who need what you have, the goal is

00:05:46.654 --> 00:05:49.172
to sell to people who
believe what you believe.

00:05:49.172 --> 00:05:52.302
The goal is not just to
hire people who need a job,

00:05:52.302 --> 00:05:55.754
it's to hire people who
believe what you believe.

00:05:55.754 --> 00:05:59.587
I always say that if you,

00:06:00.912 --> 00:06:02.722
hire people just because they can do a job

00:06:02.722 --> 00:06:03.905
they'll work for your money,

00:06:03.905 --> 00:06:05.523
but if you hire people who
believe what you believe

00:06:05.523 --> 00:06:07.825
they work for you with
blood, and sweat, and tears.

00:06:07.825 --> 00:06:10.356
And nowhere else is there
a better example of this

00:06:10.356 --> 00:06:11.961
than with the Wright brothers.

00:06:11.961 --> 00:06:15.211
Most people don't know about
Samuel Pierpont Langley.

00:06:15.211 --> 00:06:17.374
And back in the early 20th century,

00:06:17.374 --> 00:06:19.684
the pursuit of powered man flight

00:06:19.684 --> 00:06:22.569
was like the dotcom of the
day, everybody was trying it.

00:06:22.569 --> 00:06:24.380
And Samuel Pierpont Langley had

00:06:24.380 --> 00:06:28.355
what we assume to be
the recipe for success.

00:06:28.355 --> 00:06:31.220
I mean, even now we ask
people why did your product

00:06:31.220 --> 00:06:33.205
or why did your company fail,
and people always give you

00:06:33.205 --> 00:06:36.192
the same permutation of
the same three things.

00:06:36.192 --> 00:06:39.578
Under-capitalized, the wrong
people, bad market conditions,

00:06:39.578 --> 00:06:41.470
it's always the same three things.

00:06:41.470 --> 00:06:43.257
So let's explore that.

00:06:43.257 --> 00:06:46.759
Samuel Pierpont Langley was given $50,000

00:06:46.759 --> 00:06:49.616
by the war department to
figure out this flying machine.

00:06:49.616 --> 00:06:51.403
Money was no problem.

00:06:51.403 --> 00:06:54.539
He held a seat at Harvard,
and worked at the Smithsonian,

00:06:54.539 --> 00:06:56.401
and was extremely well-connected.

00:06:56.401 --> 00:06:58.350
He knew all the big minds of the day.

00:06:58.350 --> 00:07:01.996
He hired the best minds money could find

00:07:01.996 --> 00:07:04.579
and the market conditions were fantastic.

00:07:04.579 --> 00:07:07.042
The New York Times followed
him around everywhere,

00:07:07.042 --> 00:07:09.354
and everyone was rooting for Langley.

00:07:09.354 --> 00:07:13.195
Then how come we've never heard
of Samuel Pierpont Langley?

00:07:13.195 --> 00:07:16.112
A few hundred miles away in Dayton, Ohio,

00:07:16.112 --> 00:07:17.897
Orville and Wilbur Wright.

00:07:17.897 --> 00:07:20.582
They had none of what we consider

00:07:20.582 --> 00:07:22.213
to be the recipe for success.

00:07:22.213 --> 00:07:23.856
They had no money, they
paid for their dream

00:07:23.856 --> 00:07:26.399
with the proceeds from their bicycle shop.

00:07:26.399 --> 00:07:28.944
Not a single person on
the Wright brothers' team

00:07:28.944 --> 00:07:32.281
had a college education,
not even Orville or Wilbur,

00:07:32.281 --> 00:07:36.012
and the New York Times
followed them around nowhere.

00:07:36.012 --> 00:07:38.539
The difference was Orville and Wilbur

00:07:38.539 --> 00:07:41.424
were driven by a cause,
by a purpose, by a belief.

00:07:41.424 --> 00:07:43.415
They believed that if they could

00:07:43.415 --> 00:07:45.496
figure out this flying machine,

00:07:45.496 --> 00:07:48.747
it'll change the course of the world.

00:07:48.747 --> 00:07:50.831
Samuel Pierpont Langley was different.

00:07:50.831 --> 00:07:53.330
He wanted to be rich and
he wanted to be famous.

00:07:53.330 --> 00:07:55.647
He was in pursuit of the result,

00:07:55.647 --> 00:07:57.634
he was in pursuit of the riches.

00:07:57.634 --> 00:07:59.710
And lo and behold, look what happened.

00:07:59.710 --> 00:08:02.932
The people who believed in
the Wright brothers' dream

00:08:02.932 --> 00:08:05.566
worked with them with
blood, and sweat, and tears.

00:08:05.566 --> 00:08:07.759
The others just worked for the paycheck.

00:08:07.759 --> 00:08:10.136
And they tell stories of how
every time the Wright brothers

00:08:10.136 --> 00:08:12.223
went out, they would have
to take five sets of parts,

00:08:12.223 --> 00:08:14.641
because that's how many
times they would crash

00:08:14.641 --> 00:08:16.078
before they came in for supper.

00:08:16.078 --> 00:08:17.419
(audience members laughing)

00:08:17.419 --> 00:08:21.156
And eventually, on December 17, 1903,

00:08:21.156 --> 00:08:23.494
the Wright brothers took flight

00:08:23.494 --> 00:08:26.227
and no one was there
to even experience it.

00:08:26.227 --> 00:08:29.343
We found out about it a few days later.

00:08:29.343 --> 00:08:31.161
And further proof that Langley

00:08:31.161 --> 00:08:33.436
was motivated by the wrong thing,

00:08:33.436 --> 00:08:36.909
the day the Wright brothers
took flight, he quit.

00:08:36.909 --> 00:08:40.178
He could've said, "That's
an amazing discovery, guys.

00:08:40.178 --> 00:08:43.657
"Now I will improve upon your
technology," but he didn't.

00:08:43.657 --> 00:08:45.163
He wasn't first, he didn't get rich,

00:08:45.163 --> 00:08:48.269
he didn't get famous, so he quit.

00:08:48.269 --> 00:08:50.592
People don't buy what you
do, they buy why you do it.

00:08:50.592 --> 00:08:52.708
And if you talk about what you believe,

00:08:52.708 --> 00:08:55.594
you will attract those who
believe what you believe.

00:08:55.594 --> 00:08:58.044
People often say that they
think they have their Why.

00:08:58.044 --> 00:09:00.622
Well, more than that that they feel it.

00:09:00.622 --> 00:09:02.259
That it feels right and they
know they're in the ballpark,

00:09:02.259 --> 00:09:04.372
but they're struggling to find the words.

00:09:04.372 --> 00:09:05.667
That's okay.

00:09:05.667 --> 00:09:07.861
The most important part
about this Why process,

00:09:07.861 --> 00:09:10.379
is that, indeed, it feels right.

00:09:10.379 --> 00:09:12.754
Remember, the Why exists
in the part of the brain,

00:09:12.754 --> 00:09:15.661
the limbic brain that
controls our feelings.

00:09:15.661 --> 00:09:18.513
It also controls all our
behavior and decision making,

00:09:18.513 --> 00:09:21.114
and so we don't want to
"think" that it's right,

00:09:21.114 --> 00:09:22.615
we want to feel that it's right.

00:09:22.615 --> 00:09:25.386
And once you have that gut
decision, that that's it,

00:09:25.386 --> 00:09:28.671
and you get goosebumps when
you start telling the stories

00:09:28.671 --> 00:09:31.452
that you think push you towards your Why,

00:09:31.452 --> 00:09:32.442
then you know you have it.

00:09:32.442 --> 00:09:34.674
If you start to well up or
have an emotional response,

00:09:34.674 --> 00:09:37.222
you definitely know
you're in the ballpark.

00:09:37.222 --> 00:09:38.920
Then the trick is to find the words.

00:09:38.920 --> 00:09:40.699
And it's the words, it's the ability

00:09:40.699 --> 00:09:43.688
to articulate your Why
that makes it actionable.

00:09:43.688 --> 00:09:46.522
And so, absolutely, the words will evolve.

00:09:46.522 --> 00:09:50.063
The words will adapt and
change, but the meaning cannot.

00:09:50.063 --> 00:09:51.771
It's the meaning of those words,

00:09:51.771 --> 00:09:53.305
those words that give you
that visceral feeling,

00:09:53.305 --> 00:09:55.185
those goosebumps that make you well up.

00:09:55.185 --> 00:09:57.923
Whatever the feeling you had
is, that's what's paramount.

00:09:57.923 --> 00:09:59.827
But the words you can tweak and change,

00:09:59.827 --> 00:10:01.546
absolutely, for the end of time.

00:10:01.546 --> 00:10:03.981
I've tweaked my Why multiple
times over the years,

00:10:03.981 --> 00:10:05.716
but the meaning of it
and the basic direction

00:10:05.716 --> 00:10:07.408
is still exactly the same.

00:10:07.408 --> 00:10:08.717
It's like a submarine.

00:10:08.717 --> 00:10:10.839
When the captain orders
the submarine to go north,

00:10:10.839 --> 00:10:13.064
the submarine doesn't
go in a straight line,

00:10:13.064 --> 00:10:15.657
it kind of goes like this, and
your Why is the same thing.

00:10:15.657 --> 00:10:17.851
You need to know the
direction it's heading,

00:10:17.851 --> 00:10:19.610
but it's okay if it goes like this

00:10:19.610 --> 00:10:20.665
as you're tweaking the words,

00:10:20.665 --> 00:10:22.484
as long as you're moving
in the right direction.

00:10:22.484 --> 00:10:24.787
Learning to start with Why really is,

00:10:24.787 --> 00:10:26.330
especially in a business context,

00:10:26.330 --> 00:10:27.636
when you're interviewing for a job

00:10:27.636 --> 00:10:29.412
or pitching your services to someone,

00:10:29.412 --> 00:10:31.048
it really is remarkable.

00:10:31.048 --> 00:10:32.605
I get so many letters from people,

00:10:32.605 --> 00:10:34.840
all excited that they went into a pitch

00:10:34.840 --> 00:10:36.806
or they went into a job interview
and they started with Why,

00:10:36.806 --> 00:10:39.436
and they were so excited that
not only did they get the job,

00:10:39.436 --> 00:10:41.804
but the person who hired
them actually said,

00:10:41.804 --> 00:10:44.168
"We love," dot dot dot,

00:10:44.168 --> 00:10:46.418
and they repeat their Why back to them.

00:10:46.418 --> 00:10:48.253
They don't talk about their qualifications

00:10:48.253 --> 00:10:49.391
or any of these things.

00:10:49.391 --> 00:10:51.393
In other words, it really
does drive human behavior.

00:10:51.393 --> 00:10:54.340
And the more you get good at
it, the more you start to feel

00:10:54.340 --> 00:10:57.975
and see the results of starting with Why.

00:10:57.975 --> 00:11:00.458
What you'll find is that

00:11:00.458 --> 00:11:03.624
the better you are at
communicating your Why,

00:11:03.624 --> 00:11:06.408
people will want to work for you

00:11:06.408 --> 00:11:08.645
regardless of the opportunity
that you afford them.

00:11:08.645 --> 00:11:10.326
Like, they want to be a part of it.

00:11:10.326 --> 00:11:12.465
We do a little thing,
which we've been doing

00:11:12.465 --> 00:11:15.059
for years and years and
years, called a give-and-take.

00:11:15.059 --> 00:11:16.117
Whenever there's any kind of relationship,

00:11:16.117 --> 00:11:18.845
whether it's an outside partnership

00:11:18.845 --> 00:11:21.023
or even somebody who joins our team,

00:11:21.023 --> 00:11:22.438
we do something called a give-and-take,

00:11:22.438 --> 00:11:24.104
where we want somebody to be selfish

00:11:24.104 --> 00:11:26.433
and selfless within the relationship.

00:11:26.433 --> 00:11:28.368
So not give-and-get, but give-and-take.

00:11:28.368 --> 00:11:31.441
So we'll ask them, "What is it
that you have to give to us,

00:11:31.441 --> 00:11:33.785
"that you have that you
think that we need,"

00:11:33.785 --> 00:11:34.633
and they'll tell us.

00:11:34.633 --> 00:11:35.706
And then we'll say, "Great.

00:11:35.706 --> 00:11:37.735
"what is it that you
selfishly want from us?"

00:11:37.735 --> 00:11:38.626
And we want them to tell us

00:11:38.626 --> 00:11:39.719
what they can get from us and what else.

00:11:39.719 --> 00:11:40.580
- I believe in that so much, man.

00:11:40.580 --> 00:11:41.708
- And when those things match,

00:11:41.708 --> 00:11:44.101
you have a balanced relationship.

00:11:44.101 --> 00:11:46.273
Well example, I've had it with people.

00:11:46.273 --> 00:11:47.939
They'll tell me what they have to offer,

00:11:47.939 --> 00:11:49.622
and that's awesome because
that's what I want,

00:11:49.622 --> 00:11:51.197
and then they'll say what they have,

00:11:51.197 --> 00:11:52.096
what they want to take and they go,

00:11:52.096 --> 00:11:53.690
"Oh, I want to work with smart people."

00:11:53.690 --> 00:11:55.433
I'm like, "Plenty of smart people.

00:11:55.433 --> 00:11:57.122
"What is it you want to take from me?"

00:11:57.122 --> 00:11:59.026
They're like, "Oh, I want
to help build something."

00:11:59.026 --> 00:12:00.751
"Wonderful, do that anywhere.

00:12:00.751 --> 00:12:02.992
"What do you want to
take selfishly from me

00:12:02.992 --> 00:12:04.021
"that you can get nowhere else?"

00:12:04.021 --> 00:12:05.551
And if they can't answer the question,

00:12:05.551 --> 00:12:07.051
I won't engage in a relationship,

00:12:07.051 --> 00:12:09.046
and the reason is because, in time,

00:12:09.046 --> 00:12:10.088
the relationship is unbalanced.

00:12:10.088 --> 00:12:11.192
They're going to be giving,
but they're not taking,

00:12:11.192 --> 00:12:12.889
and I don't even know how
to give them what they want.

00:12:12.889 --> 00:12:15.375
Then they'll complain they're
not making enough money or,

00:12:15.375 --> 00:12:16.528
because it's not balanced.
- That's right.

00:12:16.528 --> 00:12:19.478
- Now it's time to put
your Why into action.

00:12:19.478 --> 00:12:22.461
Discovering your Why
is just the beginning.

00:12:22.461 --> 00:12:24.273
In order to enjoy all the benefits

00:12:24.273 --> 00:12:26.578
of having a clearly articulated Why,

00:12:26.578 --> 00:12:30.615
you'll need to have the courage
and discipline to use it.

00:12:30.615 --> 00:12:31.780
Like Thomas Edison said,

00:12:31.780 --> 00:12:35.202
"Vision without execution
is hallucination."

00:12:35.202 --> 00:12:37.955
There is an ideal order
for implementing your Why,

00:12:37.955 --> 00:12:40.772
though, sometimes reality
does get in the way,

00:12:40.772 --> 00:12:43.032
and it all starts with you.

00:12:43.032 --> 00:12:45.943
Our natural tendency is to
start with the tangible.

00:12:45.943 --> 00:12:48.477
We define our value by what we do,

00:12:48.477 --> 00:12:50.886
so it takes practice to start with Why.

00:12:50.886 --> 00:12:54.675
Like riding a bicycle, at
first we're unsure, unsteady.

00:12:54.675 --> 00:12:56.131
We're in our heads, thinking about

00:12:56.131 --> 00:12:57.519
all the things we need to do,

00:12:57.519 --> 00:13:00.549
pedal fast, keep enough
speed so we don't fall over.

00:13:00.549 --> 00:13:02.759
We have to really concentrate.

00:13:02.759 --> 00:13:05.330
We may even fall over,
even scrape our knees,

00:13:05.330 --> 00:13:08.138
but we get back on the bike and try again,

00:13:08.138 --> 00:13:11.037
and eventually, it becomes natural.

00:13:11.037 --> 00:13:12.541
Starting with Why is no different.

00:13:12.541 --> 00:13:14.898
At first, it feels awkward.

00:13:14.898 --> 00:13:17.065
It may not even work, but with practice

00:13:17.065 --> 00:13:20.042
it will become so natural
that you won't even be able

00:13:20.042 --> 00:13:22.721
to imagine a time when you couldn't do it.

00:13:22.721 --> 00:13:25.238
Just like riding a bicycle.

00:13:25.238 --> 00:13:27.782
In time, your Why will act as a filter

00:13:27.782 --> 00:13:30.256
for many of the decisions
and choices you make.

00:13:30.256 --> 00:13:32.352
It becomes a tool to help you find a job

00:13:32.352 --> 00:13:34.452
or seize an opportunity in which

00:13:34.452 --> 00:13:36.832
you're more likely to succeed.

00:13:36.832 --> 00:13:39.058
It removes a lot of the guessing.

00:13:39.058 --> 00:13:41.125
Here's a metaphor to show you what I mean.

00:13:41.125 --> 00:13:43.423
It's called the celery test.

00:13:43.423 --> 00:13:46.121
We're constantly asking
people for their advice

00:13:46.121 --> 00:13:48.185
on what to do or how to do it.

00:13:48.185 --> 00:13:50.741
It's like going to a dinner
party and somebody says,

00:13:50.741 --> 00:13:52.218
"Do you know what you need?

00:13:52.218 --> 00:13:53.600
"You need M&amp;M'S.

00:13:53.600 --> 00:13:56.594
"We've done so well with M&amp;M'S,
you've got to use M&amp;M'S."

00:13:56.594 --> 00:13:59.040
Somebody else says to us, "Rice milk.

00:13:59.040 --> 00:14:01.784
"In this economy, you
have to use rice milk."

00:14:01.784 --> 00:14:03.921
Someone else says to us, "KIT KATs.

00:14:03.921 --> 00:14:05.703
"You have to use KIT KATs."

00:14:05.703 --> 00:14:08.868
And somebody else says to
you, "It's all about celery."

00:14:08.868 --> 00:14:11.113
We go to the supermarket
with all this good advice,

00:14:11.113 --> 00:14:14.004
from all these smart people
with brilliant case studies,

00:14:14.004 --> 00:14:15.055
and we buy everything.

00:14:15.055 --> 00:14:18.335
We buy KIT KATs and M&amp;M'S,
celery, and rice milk.

00:14:18.335 --> 00:14:20.642
There's a lot of time we
spend at the supermarket,

00:14:20.642 --> 00:14:22.872
and a lot of money we
spend at the supermarket.

00:14:22.872 --> 00:14:25.015
And when we get to the checkout lane,

00:14:25.015 --> 00:14:26.879
we're standing there with all
these products in our hands,

00:14:26.879 --> 00:14:29.340
and no one can see what we believe

00:14:29.340 --> 00:14:31.661
because we bought everything.

00:14:31.661 --> 00:14:33.446
But let's imagine we know our Why.

00:14:33.446 --> 00:14:35.858
Let's imagine our Why
is to always be healthy,

00:14:35.858 --> 00:14:38.790
and only do things that protect
the health of our bodies.

00:14:38.790 --> 00:14:40.988
Now which products do we buy?

00:14:40.988 --> 00:14:43.345
Given all the same advice,
from all the same smart people.

00:14:43.345 --> 00:14:47.109
This time we only buy celery
and we only buy rice milk.

00:14:47.109 --> 00:14:49.075
They're the only two that make sense.

00:14:49.075 --> 00:14:51.918
We spend less time and less
money at the supermarket,

00:14:51.918 --> 00:14:53.766
and when we're standing there in line

00:14:53.766 --> 00:14:55.631
with only celery and only rice milk,

00:14:55.631 --> 00:14:58.545
now people can see what we believe.

00:14:58.545 --> 00:15:00.017
Somebody walking past can say,

00:15:00.017 --> 00:15:02.428
"Hey, I can see that
you're healthy, so am I."

00:15:02.428 --> 00:15:04.270
You just attracted an opportunity,

00:15:04.270 --> 00:15:06.235
or a referral, or a friend,

00:15:06.235 --> 00:15:09.197
simply by saying and doing
the things that you believe.

00:15:09.197 --> 00:15:11.892
And the best part is it's scalable.

00:15:11.892 --> 00:15:13.592
As soon as I said the Why,

00:15:13.592 --> 00:15:17.059
you knew exactly which
products we were going to buy.

00:15:17.059 --> 00:15:19.842
This means the more you
can articulate your Why,

00:15:19.842 --> 00:15:22.329
the more others will
know what you stand for

00:15:22.329 --> 00:15:24.802
and will be able to help you
make the right decisions.

00:15:24.802 --> 00:15:26.979
From now on, you will work to ensure

00:15:26.979 --> 00:15:28.990
everything you do is a good fit.

00:15:28.990 --> 00:15:32.137
If you do too many things
that aren't a good fit,

00:15:32.137 --> 00:15:33.882
you'll feel uncomfortable

00:15:33.882 --> 00:15:36.592
and people will feel that
you're being inauthentic.

00:15:36.592 --> 00:15:39.678
On the other hand, when you start with Why

00:15:39.678 --> 00:15:42.470
your ability to stand out, find support,

00:15:42.470 --> 00:15:46.464
and work to all your natural
strengths will flourish.

00:15:46.464 --> 00:15:49.593
With practice, you will
learn to trust your Why.

00:15:49.593 --> 00:15:51.986
You will eventually start to see your job

00:15:51.986 --> 00:15:52.979
and the things you do

00:15:52.979 --> 00:15:55.779
as ways to breathe life into your cause.

00:15:55.779 --> 00:15:58.209
And the better you get at
it, the more you will feel

00:15:58.209 --> 00:16:02.044
that your life and everything
you do has purpose.

00:16:02.044 --> 00:16:05.532
The best way to implement your
Why is to work at it slowly.

00:16:05.532 --> 00:16:08.237
You don't have to do
all the tips we suggest.

00:16:08.237 --> 00:16:11.180
What is important is
that you pick up to three

00:16:11.180 --> 00:16:14.849
and commit to practicing
and using them now.

00:16:14.849 --> 00:16:16.123
- Thank you, guys, so much for watching!

00:16:16.123 --> 00:16:17.467
I'd love to know, what did you think?

00:16:17.467 --> 00:16:19.500
What did you take from
this video from Simon

00:16:19.500 --> 00:16:21.551
that you're going to
immediately apply somehow

00:16:21.551 --> 00:16:23.177
to your life or to your business?

00:16:23.177 --> 00:16:24.887
Please leave it down
in the comments below.

00:16:24.887 --> 00:16:27.049
I'm really excited to see
what you guys have to say.

00:16:27.049 --> 00:16:28.385
Thank you guys, again, for watching.

00:16:28.385 --> 00:16:29.441
I believe in you,

00:16:29.441 --> 00:16:31.503
I hope you continue to
believe in yourself.

00:16:31.503 --> 00:16:34.925
And whatever your one word is, much love.

00:16:34.925 --> 00:16:36.425
I'll see you soon.

